# The Leak That Wasn't Where I Was Looking: Three Days Debugging a Node.js Memory Leak in Production

## 11:47 PM on a Tuesday

The PagerDuty alert hit my phone while I was brushing my teeth. I remember this because I got toothpaste on the screen trying to swipe it open.

```
[CRITICAL] cart-service: Memory usage exceeded 1.4GB threshold (node-prod-cart-07)
```

I pulled up Datadog on my laptop, still in boxers, and watched the memory graph for our cart microservice. It looked like a staircase drawn by someone with trembling hands — a slow, jagged climb over the past six hours, from a healthy ~400MB baseline to 1.4GB and rising. Kubernetes had already restarted the pod twice that day. I hadn't noticed because the restarts were masking the problem, buying us a few hours of headroom each time before the climb resumed.

I restarted the pod manually, told myself I'd look at it in the morning, and went to bed.

That was my first mistake. Not the going-to-bed part — the telling-myself-it-could-wait part.

## Wednesday Morning: The Theory That Felt So Right

By 9 AM, three more pods had tripped the memory threshold. This wasn't a single bad instance. Every replica of `cart-service` was leaking.

My immediate theory — the one I was *sure* about, the one I wasted most of Wednesday on — was that our recent migration to a new Redis client library was the culprit. We'd swapped `ioredis` for a newer wrapper library called `redis-om` about ten days earlier, and I vaguely remembered that the memory creep had started "around then." I say "vaguely" because I didn't actually check the deploy timeline against the Datadog graphs. I just *felt* like the timing was right.

I want to be clear about what happened next, because it's embarrassing: I spent roughly five hours reading `redis-om` source code, writing a reproduction script that hammered a local Redis instance with cart-shaped data, and monitoring the heap of my test process with `process.memoryUsage()`. I even opened a GitHub issue on the `redis-om` repo titled "Possible memory leak with repeated entity saves" before I had any actual evidence. (I deleted it later. I hope.)

The reproduction script showed flat memory. Completely flat. I told myself this was because my local test "wasn't realistic enough" and kept going for another hour, adding more concurrent connections, bigger payloads, longer durations. Still flat.

At around 3 PM, my colleague Priya pinged me on Slack:

> **Priya**: hey did you check when the memory thing actually started? I'm looking at the graphs and it doesn't line up with the redis-om deploy at all

She was right. The `redis-om` migration had deployed on the 4th. The memory creep started on the 8th. Four days apart. I had burned five hours chasing a library that had been running clean in production for four days before anything went wrong.

I stared at the Datadog graph with the deploy markers overlaid and felt the specific kind of stupid that comes from not doing the obvious thing first.

## Wednesday Afternoon: Actually Looking at the Heap

With my Redis theory in the garbage where it belonged, I did what I should have done at 9 AM: I took a heap snapshot of a live pod.

We had `--inspect` disabled in production (as you do), so I had to deploy a canary with the inspector enabled, port-forward through `kubectl`, and connect Chrome DevTools to the remote process. This took about 40 minutes of fumbling with the right combination of flags, because I always forget the exact `kubectl port-forward` syntax and end up connecting to the wrong port at least once.

The first heap snapshot, taken about 20 minutes after the canary pod started, showed 410MB of heap used. Unremarkable. I took another one 90 minutes later: 680MB. The leak was active.

I loaded both snapshots into Chrome DevTools and used the "Comparison" view. The retained size delta was dominated by one thing: **strings**. Millions of small strings, most of them looking like fragments of JSON. The "Objects allocated between Snapshot 1 and Snapshot 2" view showed a massive number of `(string)` entries retained by objects I didn't immediately recognize.

Here's where I made my second wrong turn, though this one was shorter. I assumed we had a logging problem — that we were somehow buffering log output in memory instead of streaming it. We use `pino` for structured logging, and I spent about 45 minutes reading through our logging configuration, checking that we weren't accidentally holding references to log entries. We weren't. Pino was fine. Pino is always fine.

But the strings *did* look like log output. They had timestamps, they had JSON structure, they had field names I recognized from our request context. I just couldn't figure out what was holding onto them.

## Thursday Morning: The Clue I Almost Missed

I came in Thursday with fresh eyes and a large coffee and went back to the heap comparison. This time, instead of looking at the strings themselves, I followed the retainer chain — clicking through the "Retainers" panel in DevTools to see *what was holding references* to these strings.

The chain went:

```
(string) → Object → Array → Object → EventEmitter → ...
```

That `EventEmitter` caught my eye. I expanded it. The retainer was an instance of our custom `RequestTracer` class — a piece of middleware I'd written myself three months earlier to correlate distributed traces across services.

My stomach dropped a little.

I clicked deeper. The `RequestTracer` instance was holding an array called `_spans`. And that array, in the 90-minute-old snapshot, had over 40,000 entries.

I opened the `RequestTracer` source file. It was 80 lines of code. I'd written every one of them. Here's the relevant section, stripped down:

```javascript
class RequestTracer {
  constructor() {
    this._spans = [];
  }

  startSpan(name, metadata) {
    const span = {
      name,
      metadata,  // <-- this included the full serialized request context
      startTime: Date.now(),
    };
    this._spans.push(span);
    return span;
  }

  endSpan(span) {
    span.endTime = Date.now();
    span.duration = span.endTime - span.startTime;
    // That's it. That's the whole method.
    // No cleanup. No removal from this._spans. Nothing.
  }
}
```

Do you see it? `endSpan` marks the span as complete but never removes it from the `_spans` array. Every completed span stays in memory forever.

But wait — this class was instantiated per-request in our Express middleware. Each request got its own `RequestTracer`, and when the request ended, the tracer should have been garbage collected along with everything else in the request scope. So why was it leaking?

This is where the story gets good, and by "good" I mean "I wanted to crawl under my desk."

## Thursday Afternoon: The Real Root Cause

The `RequestTracer` wasn't leaking because of the `_spans` array. That was a code smell, sure, but it wasn't the actual problem. The actual problem was *how* the tracer was being registered.

Three weeks earlier — on the 8th, the exact day the memory creep started, as I would later confirm with a `git log --after` that made me want to scream — I had added a feature to emit tracer summaries to our internal metrics pipeline. To do this, I'd registered a listener on a process-level event emitter:

```javascript
// In the request middleware
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const tracer = new RequestTracer();
  req.tracer = tracer;

  // THIS. THIS RIGHT HERE.
  metricsEmitter.on('flush', () => {
    tracer.summarize().forEach(summary => {
      metricsEmitter.emit('metric', summary);
    });
  });

  next();
});
```

`metricsEmitter` was a module-level singleton. It lived for the entire lifetime of the process. Every incoming request registered a new anonymous closure on it — a closure that captured `tracer` in its scope. The `flush` event fired every 30 seconds, and when it did, it called *every listener ever registered*, including the ones from requests that had finished hours ago.

The listeners were never removed. The closures kept the `RequestTracer` instances alive. The tracers kept the `_spans` arrays alive. The spans kept the serialized request metadata alive. That's where all those strings were coming from.

Every request that hit our service permanently added ~2KB of retained memory that would never be freed until the process died.

At peak traffic — about 800 requests per second — that's roughly 1.6MB per second of permanent memory growth. The staircase graph suddenly made perfect, horrible sense. The "steps" corresponded to traffic spikes during business hours, and the "flats" were overnight lulls.

I sat there for a minute, just looking at the three lines of code. I had written them. I had reviewed my own PR (we were short-staffed that week, which is an explanation, not an excuse). I had deployed them. And I had spent a day blaming a Redis library for my own event listener leak.

## The Fix

The fix was almost anticlimactic. Two changes:

First, I moved the metrics emission out of the per-request middleware and into the `RequestTracer` itself, triggered on response finish:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const tracer = new RequestTracer();
  req.tracer = tracer;

  res.on('finish', () => {
    // Emit metrics once, when the request is done, then let go
    const summaries = tracer.summarize();
    summaries.forEach(s => metricsEmitter.emit('metric', s));
    tracer.dispose(); // new method: clears _spans, breaks references
  });

  next();
});
```

Second, I added the `dispose()` method to `RequestTracer` to explicitly clear the spans array, because I no longer trusted implicit garbage collection to save me from myself:

```javascript
dispose() {
  this._spans = null;
}
```

I deployed the fix Thursday evening. By Friday morning, the Datadog memory graph was flat. A beautiful, boring horizontal line at 380MB. I have never been so happy to see a boring graph.

## What I Actually Changed About How I Work

I'm not going to tell you to "always clean up your event listeners" or "monitor your memory usage." You know that. I knew that. Knowing it didn't stop me from writing the bug or from spending a day chasing the wrong cause.

Here's what I actually do differently now:

**I check deploy timelines before I check source code.** The very first thing I do when investigating a production issue is overlay deploy markers on the metrics graph in Datadog. Not after I have a theory — *before*. If I'd done this on Wednesday morning, I would have eliminated the Redis library in thirty seconds instead of five hours. I have a sticky note on my monitor that says "WHEN DID IT START" in block letters. It's been there for four months and I'm not taking it down.

**I grep for `.on(` in PR reviews now.** Specifically, I look for event listener registrations inside request handlers or loops — anywhere a long-lived emitter might accumulate listeners from short-lived contexts. This is the specific pattern that bit me: a module-scoped emitter gaining a listener per request. I added a custom ESLint rule that flags `.on(` calls inside Express/Koa middleware functions. It's caught two near-misses since.

**I take heap snapshots in pairs, not singles.** A single heap snapshot is almost useless for leak detection. The comparison view between two snapshots taken 30-60 minutes apart is where the signal is. I wrote a small shell script that automates the "connect to pod, take snapshot, wait, take another, download both" workflow so I don't have to fumble with `kubectl port-forward` flags every time. The script is 20 lines of bash and has saved me hours.

**I stopped self-reviewing PRs.** This one's organizational, not technical. The week I wrote the leaking code, I was the only one available to review it, so I approved my own PR. I now block-merge my own PRs even if it means waiting a day for someone else to look. The bug was three lines of code in an 80-line file. Any second pair of eyes would have caught the closure over `tracer` being registered on a singleton emitter. I was too close to it, thinking about the feature I was building instead of the lifecycle of the objects I was creating.

---

The total time from first alert to deployed fix was about 44 hours. Roughly 8 of those were sleeping, 6 were spent on the Redis dead end, and maybe 3 were actual productive investigation. The rest was meetings, context-switching, and staring at graphs while drinking coffee.

The leak itself was three lines of JavaScript. It always is.

# How We Fixed a Memory Leak in Our Node.js Microservice

## Introduction

Memory leaks are one of those things every developer deals with at some point. Recently, our team encountered a memory leak in one of our Node.js microservices that took us a while to figure out. In this post, I'll walk you through how we debugged it and what we learned.

## The Problem

We started getting alerts that our order-processing microservice was consuming more memory than usual. The service would start at around 200MB of heap usage and gradually climb until it hit the container limit and got OOM-killed by Kubernetes. This was happening roughly every 18 hours.

We knew we had a memory leak. The question was where.

## Initial Investigation

The first thing I did was look at our monitoring dashboard. The memory graph showed a clear upward trend — a classic sawtooth pattern where garbage collection would reclaim some memory but never bring it back to baseline. Each cycle left a little more residual memory behind.

I had a few theories:

1. Maybe it was related to our recent dependency upgrade
2. Could be a connection pool issue with our database
3. Possibly an event listener leak

I started by checking the dependency upgrade since that was the most recent change. We had bumped `express` from 4.17 to 4.18 and updated a few other packages. I spent some time looking through changelogs but didn't find anything suspicious.

## Finding the Cause

After ruling out the dependency upgrade, I decided to take heap snapshots. I used Chrome DevTools to connect to the running process and captured three snapshots at 5-minute intervals.

Looking at the heap snapshots, I noticed that the number of retained objects was growing. Specifically, there were a lot of objects related to our caching layer. We had an in-memory cache that stored processed order results for deduplication purposes.

Here's what the problematic code looked like:

```javascript
const orderCache = {};

app.post('/process-order', async (req, res) => {
  const orderId = req.body.orderId;

  if (orderCache[orderId]) {
    return res.json({ status: 'already_processed' });
  }

  const result = await processOrder(req.body);
  orderCache[orderId] = { result, timestamp: Date.now() };

  res.json({ status: 'processed', result });
});
```

The problem is pretty obvious when you see it: we were adding entries to `orderCache` but never removing them. Every order that came through got cached forever. With thousands of orders per hour, this object just kept growing.

## The Fix

The fix was straightforward. We added a TTL-based eviction mechanism:

```javascript
const orderCache = new Map();
const CACHE_TTL = 60 * 60 * 1000; // 1 hour

function cleanupCache() {
  const now = Date.now();
  for (const [key, value] of orderCache) {
    if (now - value.timestamp > CACHE_TTL) {
      orderCache.delete(key);
    }
  }
}

setInterval(cleanupCache, 5 * 60 * 1000); // Clean every 5 minutes

app.post('/process-order', async (req, res) => {
  const orderId = req.body.orderId;

  if (orderCache.has(orderId)) {
    return res.json({ status: 'already_processed' });
  }

  const result = await processOrder(req.body);
  orderCache.set(orderId, { result, timestamp: Date.now() });

  res.json({ status: 'processed', result });
});
```

We also considered using a proper caching solution like Redis, but for our use case the in-memory approach with TTL was sufficient.

After deploying the fix, memory usage stabilized and we haven't had an OOM kill since.

## Lessons Learned

Here are some takeaways from this experience:

1. **Always monitor your memory usage.** If we hadn't had alerts set up, this could have gone unnoticed for much longer.

2. **Be careful with in-memory caches.** Any time you're storing data in memory, make sure you have an eviction strategy. Unbounded caches are a common source of memory leaks.

3. **Heap snapshots are your friend.** Chrome DevTools makes it relatively easy to inspect what's consuming memory in a Node.js process. Learn to use this tool before you need it.

4. **Code review matters.** This cache was added in a quick PR that didn't get thorough review. A second pair of eyes probably would have caught the missing eviction logic.

5. **Keep your debugging tools ready.** Having the ability to connect to production processes for debugging saved us a lot of time.

## Conclusion

Memory leaks can be tricky, but with the right tools and a systematic approach, they're usually solvable. The key is to not panic and methodically narrow down the source. Hopefully this post helps someone else who's dealing with a similar issue in their Node.js services.

Happy debugging!

